# eHacking in-network DNS resolver (CoreDNS).
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# Containers have to reach the platform through Traefik, and Traefik can
# only be addressed by the *hostname* its routers match on — SNI and the
# Host header both carry it. Compose can make a container answer to extra
# names (the `aliases:` on traefik below), but only to a FINITE list. The
# catcher hands every student their own salt subdomain
# `<salt>.${CATCHER_HOST}`, which is an unbounded set, so that list can
# never be complete.
#
# Measured on this host before the resolver existed:
#
#     container -> getent hosts my.e-attacker.de   132.195.101.17  (correct)
#     container -> 132.195.101.17:443              REFUSED
#     container -> 132.195.101.17:10443            OPEN
#
# The name resolves to this machine's own public address, but rootless
# podman's egress traverses neither the host's nat/PREROUTING nor its
# nat/OUTPUT, so the firewall's 443->10443 redirect never applies and
# nothing answers on 443. No host firewall rule can fix that; the name has
# to resolve inside the compose network instead.
#
# The symptom was the OIDC SP's server-side discovery fetch of
# `https://<salt>.${CATCHER_HOST}/.well-known/openid-configuration`
# (AttackManager.startWebfinger) failing with `ConnectException:
# Connection refused`, which takes the whole mIdP family (ids-1..ids-4)
# with it. The same path carries out-of-band XXE and SSRF exfiltration
# from xml-sec, soap-sec, json-sec and rest-api-sec.
#
# What it does
# ------------
# One server block per zone this platform serves. DNS zone semantics do the
# "is this name in the zone" test natively: the block matches the apex and
# every subdomain at any depth, and nothing else. A name that merely ends
# with the zone text (`notmy-e-hacking.de`) or contains it in the middle
# (`e-hacking.de.evil.com`) is NOT in the zone and falls through to the
# catch-all block — verified, both return NXDOMAIN rather than traefik.
#
# The zone names come from the environment, so .env stays the single place
# they are written down. Adding a hostname under an existing zone needs no
# change here; adding a whole new zone means a new block plus a matching
# `environment:` entry on the dns service.
#
# Inside a block every name is rewritten to the *service name* `traefik`,
# not an address. The container runtime's own DNS (podman's aardvark-dns,
# Docker's embedded resolver) resolves it on every query, so this file
# needs no static IP and survives `compose up --force-recreate`.
#
# `answer auto` rewrites the owner name in the response back to the name
# the client asked for. Without it a client asking for
# `abc.e-attacker.de.` gets an answer owned by `traefik.`, which a strict
# resolver may drop as off-question.
#
# The final `.:53` block forwards everything else — service names like
# `victim-bot` or `couchdb`, and real internet names — to the runtime's own
# resolver via this container's /etc/resolv.conf. That is what keeps
# service-name resolution working for every container pointed at us, and it
# is also how the rewritten `traefik` query gets answered.
#
# Debugging: add `log` to a block and `just restart dns` to get one line per
# query. Two traps cost real time here, both the same shape: CoreDNS matches
# the FULLY QUALIFIED name, with a trailing dot. A `rewrite name regex`
# without `\.$`, and a `rewrite name suffix` without the trailing dot, both
# silently never match — which looks exactly like the resolver not being
# wired up at all.

{$ZONE_PLATFORM}:53 {
    rewrite name regex .* traefik answer auto
    forward . /etc/resolv.conf
    cache 30
    errors
}

{$ZONE_CATCHER}:53 {
    rewrite name regex .* traefik answer auto
    forward . /etc/resolv.conf
    cache 30
    errors
}

.:53 {
    forward . /etc/resolv.conf
    cache 30
    reload
    errors
}
